  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1052 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nTemperatures warmed noticeably across the forecast area Sunday \nafternoon, reaching the low to mid 50s. An upper low sliding into \nthe southeastern CONUS will allow upper ridging to dominate over the \nCentral Plains early this week. Expect afternoon highs to continue \nto climb, reaching the mid to upper 50s Monday, mid 50s to low 60s \nTuesday, and low to mid 60s on Wednesday. \n\nThe pressure gradient along the backside of the ridge will tighten \nwith an approaching shortwave by midweek. Winds will increase with \ngusts up to 30-40 mph likely, especially over northeast Nebraska. \nWhile gusty winds may increase fire danger concerns with unharvested \ndry crops, minimum relative humidities bottoming out at 40-50% \nshould help mitigate those concerns. \n\nThe previously mentioned shortwave trough will move from the west \ncoast into the northern plains by Wednesday, ushering the upper \nridge eastward. Our strong southwesterly winds will come to an end \nas this system sweeps a cold front through the Central Plains \nWednesday evening. While a few spotty showers still can't be \ncompletely ruled out, the better chance for precipitation will \nremain to our east and north. \n\nThursday and Beyond...\n\nThe focus for the forecast will shift to a stronger low moving into \nthe Pacific Northwest Thursday. By Friday morning, this system will \ninduce a surface low out of the Rockies, bringing increasing \nprecipitation chances to the region Friday-Saturday. The current \nforecast remains aligned with guidance suggesting rain showers and a \nfew thunderstorms will develop along and ahead of the surface cold \nfront associated with this system. The better cold air and chances \nfor snow will likely remain just to the north of our forecast area, \nas overall ensemble members suggest a more progressive and \nnortheastward shift of the system.",
